/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"m4l-level-10","number":10,"title":"Module 10","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"10.1","modules":[{"id":"m4l-m-10-1-1","code":"10.1","title":"10.1","blurb":"10.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-10-1-1","title":"10.1.1: Advanced MIDI — Note Processing Algorithms","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 10.1.1","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 10 / Section 10.1","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 9.3.1 - Defined Latency and Host Compensation.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Cycling '74: <em>Max 9 MIDI Guide: Note Processing, Tables, and Coll</em>.","Ableton Patch Code Standard: <em>MIDI Transformation and Edge-Case Safety</em>.","Ableton Production Guidelines: <em>Algorithmic MIDI Generation</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Construct explicit musical scale quantization lookup tables using <code>coll</code> and <code>table</code> data structures.","Implement multi-voice chord generators that expand single monophonic Note-On triggers into harmonized chord tuples with synchronous Note-Off tracking.","Build tempo-synced MIDI echo and arpeggiation delay lines using <code>pipe</code> and note value subdivisions.","Enforce edge-note boundary safety (0 pitch 127) to prevent illegal MIDI byte transmission.","Apply deterministic seeding to probabilistic MIDI note processors."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 Explicit Scale Quantization Topology","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Scale quantization maps arbitrary chromatic MIDI notes (0–127) to the nearest valid scale degrees within a designated musical key (e.g., C Major, D Dorian, F# Minor)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        SCALE QUANTIZATION LOOKUP GRAPH                        |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  [ Incoming Note: Pitch P ]                                                   |\n|             |                                                                 |\n|             v                                                                 |\n|  [ Octave &amp; Degree Split ]                                                    |\n|  - Octave Base = (P / 12) * 12                                                |\n|  - Chromatic Degree = P % 12 (0 - 11)                                         |\n|             |                                                                 |\n|             v                                                                 |\n|  +-----------------------------------+                                        |\n|  | [table ---scale_map] (0 to 11)    |  &lt;-- Maps chromatic degree to          |\n|  | 0-&gt;0, 1-&gt;0, 2-&gt;2, 3-&gt;4, 4-&gt;4...   |      quantized scale degree            |\n|  +-----------------+-----------------+                                        |\n|                    |                                                          |\n|                    v (Quantized Degree)                                       |\n|  [ Sum: Octave Base + Quantized Degree ]                                      |\n|             |                                                                 |\n|             v                                                                 |\n|  [ Quantized Pitch Out (In Scale) ]                                           |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Rule:</em> Scale maps must never be assumed or hardcoded invisibly. Always document the scale table and root tuning in the device documentation."},{"type":"heading","text":"3.2 Chord Generation and Synchronous Note-Off Tracking","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Generating a 3-note triad from a single incoming note requires tracking three distinct pitch offsets for every Note-On, and sending three corresponding Note-Off events when the key is released:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                      [ Note-On: Pitch 60, Vel 100 ]\n                                    |\n                                    v\n                           +-----------------+\n                           |     t l l l     |\n                           +----+---+---+----+\n                                |   |   |\n                   +------------+   |   +------------+\n                   |                v                |\n                   v            [ + 4 ]              v\n                [ + 0 ]       (Major 3rd)         [ + 7 ]\n                (Root)             |            (Perfect 5th)\n                   |               v                 |\n                   +-------&gt; [ noteout ] &lt;-----------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"When Note-Off (velocity <code>0</code>) arrives for pitch <code>60</code>, the device must emit Note-Off for <code>60</code>, <code>64</code>, and <code>67</code> simultaneously to prevent stuck chord tones."},{"type":"heading","text":"3.3 Tempo-Synced MIDI Echo with [pipe]","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"The <code>[pipe]</code> object delays message streams by an explicit time duration:"},{"type":"list","items":["Syntax: <code>[pipe 0 0 4n]</code> (Delays pitch and velocity tuples by a quarter note).","When combined with a velocity decay multiplier (e.g., V_echo = V × 0.7), <code>pipe</code> creates tempo-synced musical MIDI echoes."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Construct a real-time Scale Quantizer and Harmonizer MIDI effect that quantizes incoming notes to a selectable Major/Minor scale and adds a selectable musical fifth interval."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, insert a <strong>Max MIDI Effect</strong> on a track before an instrument.","Open in Max and enable <strong>Open in Presentation</strong>.","Ingest notes using <code>[notein]</code>.","Create an octave/degree decomposition network:","Pitch → <code>[trigger i i]</code> → left inlet to <code>[/ 12]</code> (calculates octave) → <code>[* 12]</code>.","Right inlet to <code>[% 12]</code> (calculates chromatic degree 0–11).","Create a <code>[table ---major_map]</code> containing C Major intervals <code>[0 0 2 2 4 4 5 5 7 7 9 11]</code>.","Route <code>% 12</code> through <code>[table ---major_map]</code>, and sum with <code>[* 12]</code> using <code>[+ 0]</code>.","Implement Harmonizer:","Create a <code>[live.toggle]</code> named <code>Harmony_Enable</code>.","When enabled, route quantized note to <code>[noteout]</code> (Root) and to <code>[+ 7]</code> → <code>[clip 0 127]</code> → <code>[noteout]</code> (Harmonic 5th).","Add <code>Harmony_Enable</code> to Presentation Mode and align on the 8x8 grid.","Save as <code>Scale_Harmonizer_Lab.amxd</code>.","In Live, play chromatic scales: Verify that all notes snap strictly to Major scale degrees and harmonize cleanly without stuck notes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Pitch Overflow Past 127:</strong> Adding transposition (+12 or +7) to high notes (e.g., MIDI note 124 + 7 = 131). MIDI note numbers above 127 are invalid and cause synthesizer crashes. Always clamp pitches using <code>[clip 0 127]</code>.","<strong>Note-Off Desynchronization in Polyphonic Streams:</strong> Storing single pitch variables in global memory during fast chord playing. If Note 60 is pressed, then Note 64 is pressed, releasing Note 60 might read Note 64's memory, leaving Note 60 hanging. Use <code>[poly]</code> or list tuples to preserve polyphonic voice binding.","<strong>Floating Point Pitches:</strong> Accidental float math resulting in fractional note numbers (<code>60.45</code>). Standard MIDI note messages require strict integers."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Self-Assessment & Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checkpoint Criteria","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To confirm mastery of this module, the student must successfully complete the following criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Diagram the mathematical decomposition of a MIDI note into its octave base and modulo chromatic degree.","[ ] Explain how <code>[pipe]</code> is configured to delay note tuples by tempo-synced musical subdivisions (<code>8nd</code>).","[ ] Demonstrate complete Note-Off integrity on a 4-note chord harmonizer during rapid legato keyboard playing."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Max 9 User Guide: MIDI Processing, Data Tables, and Coll</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: MIDI Event Transforms</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation.","Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Max 9 Reference Manual: pipe and table objects</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"m4l-m-10-2-2","code":"10.2","title":"10.2","blurb":"10.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-10-2-1","title":"10.2.1: Advanced MIDI — CC, Pressure, and SysEx Protocols","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 10.2.1","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 10 / Section 10.2","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 10.1.1 - Advanced MIDI — Note Processing Algorithms.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Ableton Knowledge Base: <em>SysEx support in Ableton Live</em>.","Cycling '74: <em>Max 9 MIDI Guide: Continuous Controllers, Channel Pressure, and Pitch Bend</em>.","Ableton Patch Code Standard: <em>Feedback Prevention in Control Streams</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Ingest, scale, filter, and remap MIDI Continuous Controller (CC) messages using <code>[ctlin]</code>, <code>[ctlout]</code>, and <code>[midiparse]</code>.","Differentiate between Channel Pressure (Monophonic Aftertouch) and Polyphonic Key Pressure.","Identify and prevent destructive feedback loops when transmitting bi-directional MIDI data to hardware control surfaces.","Articulate the precise technical constraints governing System Exclusive (SysEx) messaging within Ableton Live.","Construct a CC remapping utility with automatable parameter scaling and bypass switching."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Continuous Controllers (CC) and Pressure Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 Continuous Controller (CC) Structure","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"A standard MIDI CC message consists of three 7-bit bytes: <code>[Status Byte (0xB0 + Channel), Controller Number (0–127), Value (0–127)]</code>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                            MIDI CC ROUTING TOPOLOGY                           |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  [ ctlin ] (Listens for incoming CC from track)                               |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       +-------------------+-------------------+                               |\n|       | (Value 0-127)     | (CC Number 0-127) | (Channel 1-16)                |\n|       v                   v                   v                               |\n|  [ Scale / Remap ]   [ Filter / Select ]   [ Channel Route ]                  |\n|       |                   |                   |                               |\n|       v                   v                   v                               |\n|  [ ctlout ] (Transmits remapped CC stream back to track)                      |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"3.2 Channel Pressure vs. Polyphonic Key Pressure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Channel Pressure (<code>[touchin]</code> / <code>[touchout]</code>):</strong> A single global pressure value applied across all active voices on a MIDI channel (standard aftertouch).","<strong>Polyphonic Key Pressure (<code>[polytouchin]</code> / <code>[polytouchout]</code>):</strong> Independent pressure values sent per individual note key, allowing expressive pressure modulation on single notes within held chords."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Hardware Feedback Loop Prevention","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When a Max device communicates bi-directionally with a MIDI controller (e.g., updating motor faders or LED rings while receiving user input):"},{"type":"list","items":["If the hardware transmits CC 1 → Max updates its internal dial → Max emits CC 1 back to the hardware → the hardware echoes CC 1 back to Max.","<strong>The Result:</strong> An infinite message flood that locks the MIDI thread and crashes the host."],"ordered":false},{"type":"heading","text":"The Feedback Suppression Pattern:","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Use the <code>set</code> message to update internal UI objects without triggering their output outlet:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                   [ Incoming MIDI CC from Hardware ]\n                                  |\n                                  v\n                         +-----------------+\n                         |     set $1      |  &lt;-- Updates dial display visually\n                         +--------+--------+\n                                  |\n                                  v\n                         +-----------------+\n                         |    live.dial    |  (Emits NOTHING from outlet)\n                         +-----------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. System Exclusive (SysEx) Support Constraints in Live","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"According to the official <strong>Ableton SysEx Support Knowledge Base</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Supported Capabilities:</strong>","Max for Live devices can receive and transmit SysEx messages to external hardware ports via <code>[sysexin]</code> and <code>[sysexout]</code> or <code>[midiin]</code> / <code>[midiout]</code>.","Max for Live can communicate with external synthesizers for patch dumping and parameter editing.","<strong>Documented Limitations:</strong>","Ableton Live <strong>does not record SysEx messages into MIDI clips</strong> on the arrangement or session timeline.","SysEx streams pass through track device chains in real time but cannot be automated or edited as clip envelopes in Live's standard clip editor."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Construct a real-time CC Remapper that intercepts Expression Pedal data (CC 11), scales and curves the values, and remaps them to Filter Cutoff (CC 74) with a clean bypass switch."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, insert a <strong>Max MIDI Effect</strong> before an instrument on a MIDI track.","Open in Max and enable <strong>Open in Presentation</strong>.","Ingest CC messages: Create <code>[ctlin 11]</code> (Filters specifically for CC 11).","Create a <code>[live.dial]</code> named <code>Max_Output</code> (Float, range: <code>0 to 127</code>, default: <code>127</code>).","Create a <code>[live.toggle]</code> named <code>Bypass</code>.","Signal Pipeline:","Scale incoming CC 11 value (0–127) against <code>Max_Output</code> using <code>[* 1.0]</code> → <code>[/ 127.0]</code>.","Route through <code>[clip 0 127]</code>.","Transmit Remapped CC:","Route the scaled value into the left inlet of <code>[ctlout 74]</code> (Transmits as CC 74).","Pass-Through Gate:","When <code>Bypass</code> is enabled, open a <code>[gate]</code> to route original CC 11 directly to <code>[ctlout 11]</code>.","Add all UI widgets to Presentation Mode and align on the 8x8 grid.","Save device as <code>CC_Expression_Remapper.amxd</code>.","In Live, send CC 11 from a MIDI controller: Verify in Live's MIDI Monitor that CC 74 is emitted with scaled ranges, and toggling Bypass restores original CC 11."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Unbounded CC Floods:</strong> Routing high-rate continuous controller sweeps through un-thinned message cascades, overloading external hardware MIDI DIN ports (31.25 kbaud bandwidth limit). Use <code>[speedlim 10]</code> to throttle control rates when driving legacy hardware.","<strong>Infinite Feedback Loops:</strong> Connecting <code>[ctlin]</code> → <code>[live.dial]</code> → <code>[ctlout]</code> on the same CC number without isolating input from output.","<strong>Attempting to Automate SysEx in Live Clips:</strong> Promising users that a device will record SysEx dumps into Live arrangement clips. SysEx cannot be serialized into Live MIDI clip envelopes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Self-Assessment & Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checkpoint Criteria","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To confirm mastery of this module, the student must successfully complete the following criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Diagram the three 7-bit bytes comprising a standard MIDI Continuous Controller message.","[ ] Explain how the <code>[set $1(</code> message suppresses recursive feedback loops in bi-directional MIDI hardware bridges.","[ ] State the official capabilities and limitations of System Exclusive (SysEx) messaging inside Ableton Live 12."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"9. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton AG. (2024). <em>SysEx support in Ableton Live</em>. Ableton Technical Support Knowledge Base.","Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Max 9 MIDI Reference: ctlin, ctlout, touchin, and sysexin</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: Control Surface Communication</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"m4l-m-10-3-3","code":"10.3","title":"10.3","blurb":"10.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-10-3-1","title":"10.3.1: Advanced MIDI — Algorithmic Step Sequencing","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 10.3.1","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 10 / Section 10.3","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 10.2.1 - Advanced MIDI — CC, Pressure, and SysEx Protocols.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Cycling '74: <em>Max for Live Objects: live.step Specification</em>.","Ableton Production Guidelines: <em>Real-Time Sequencers in Device Chains</em>.","Ableton Patch Code Standard: <em>Deterministic Randomness and Seeding</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Design and construct a real-time, transport-synchronized <strong>16-Step MIDI Sequencer</strong> that operates within a track's device chain.","Differentiate between track-chain MIDI Sequencers (real-time stream generators) and Clip View MIDI Tools (non-real-time clip editors).","Utilize the <code>[live.step]</code> multi-track step sequencer UI object to edit pitch, velocity, and step duration visually.","Implement stochastic step probability algorithms using pseudo-random generators with explicit user-defined random seeds.","Guarantee that sequence playback resets and aligns with Live's downbeat upon transport start."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Real-Time Device Chain Sequencer vs. Clip MIDI Tool","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        SEQUENCER ARCHITECTURE COMPARISON                          |\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| Attribute         | Track-Chain MIDI Sequencer    | Live 12 MIDI Tool             |\n|                   | (This Lesson)                 | (Lesson 15.1.1 / Module 15)      |\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| **Execution**     | Real-time streaming during    | Offline generation /          |\n|                   | active transport playback.    | batch calculation.            |\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| **Host Boundary** | MIDI Track Device Chain       | Clip View Editor              |\n|                   | (Emits to downstream synth)   | (Writes notes into .alc clip) |\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| **I/O Objects**   | `[midiin]` / `[noteout]`      | `[live.miditool.in]` /        |\n|                   |                               | `[live.miditool.out]`         |\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. The live.step UI Object and Sequencer Engine","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"4.1 The [live.step] Multi-Lane Grid","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"The <code>[live.step]</code> object provides an integrated multi-channel sequencer interface supporting:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Pitch:</strong> Dedicated semitone step rows.","<strong>Velocity:</strong> Per-step velocity bars (0–127).","<strong>Duration:</strong> Step gate lengths (0–100%).","<strong>Extra:</strong> General-purpose modulation data lane."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                  [ Transport-Synced Metro: 16n ]\n                                 |\n                                 v\n                       +-------------------+\n                       |    counter 1 16   |  &lt;-- Advances Step Pointer 1 -&gt; 16\n                       +---------+---------+\n                                 |\n                                 v\n                       +-------------------+\n                       |     live.step     |  &lt;-- Emits [pitch, vel, dur]\n                       +---------+---------+\n                                 |\n                                 v\n                       +-------------------+\n                       |     makenote      |  &lt;-- Generates paired Note-On/Off\n                       +---------+---------+\n                                 |\n                                 v\n                       +-------------------+\n                       |      noteout      |\n                       +-------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"4.2 Deterministic Randomness & Seeding Policy","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"When building probabilistic step sequencers (e.g., Euclidean or stochastic chance generators):"},{"type":"list","items":["A standard random object (<code>[random 100]</code>) generates unpredictable sequences every time a loop repeats, preventing users from creating repeatable musical hooks.","<strong>The Solution:</strong> Implement a <strong>Random Seed</strong> control (<code>[live.dial Seed]</code>). Sending <code>seed $1</code> into <code>[random]</code> resets the pseudo-random generator's state table. When playing back with the same seed, the probabilistic pattern repeats with 100% mathematical consistency."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Build a 16-step poly-rhythmic MIDI sequencer with selectable sync rates, step probability, and transport downbeat alignment."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, insert a <strong>Max MIDI Effect</strong> before an instrument on a MIDI track.","Select <strong>Edit in Max</strong> and enable <strong>Open in Presentation</strong>.","Create a transport-locked clock:","Instantiate <code>[metro 16n @autostart 1 @active 1]</code>.","Downbeat Reset:","Create a <code>[timepoint 1.1.0]</code> object. Connect its outlet to the reset inlet (inlet 3) of a <code>[counter 1 1 16]</code>.","Connect <code>[metro]</code> to <code>[counter 1 1 16]</code>.","Instantiate <code>[live.step @nsteps 16 @mode 0]</code>. Connect the step index from <code>[counter]</code> to <code>[live.step]</code>.","Extract Note Data:","Connect outlet 1 of <code>[live.step]</code> to <code>[unpack i i i]</code> (Pitch, Velocity, Duration).","Implement Probability Gate:","Create a <code>[live.dial]</code> named <code>Probability</code> (Int, 0–100%, default: 100%).","Trigger a <code>[random 100]</code> on every step → compare with <code>[&lt; $1]</code>.","Gate velocity stream: If random value  Probability, pass velocity; else emit <code>0</code>.","Route pitch, gated velocity, and duration into <code>[makenote 60 120]</code> → <code>[noteout]</code>.","Add <code>live.step</code>, <code>Probability</code>, and a rate-select <code>live.tab</code> to Presentation Mode.","Save as <code>Step_Sequencer_Lab.amxd</code>.","Play the Live Set: Program a 16-step bassline in <code>live.step</code>, lower Probability to 75%, and confirm musical, synchronized playback that resets on every bar."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Free-Running Clocks Uncoupled from Transport:</strong> Using <code>[metro 125]</code> with millisecond timing. As tempo changes or the user stops playback, the sequencer runs out of sync with Live's bars. Always use tempo-relative note values (<code>16n</code>).","<strong>Missing <code>makenote</code> Duration Handlers:</strong> Sending raw Note-On messages without scheduling matching Note-Off events, causing permanent drone tones.","<strong>Sequencer Phase Offset on Loop Jump:</strong> Failing to reset the step counter when Live loops a region. Always listen to transport phase or downbeat points."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Self-Assessment & Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checkpoint Criteria","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To confirm mastery of this module, the student must successfully complete the following criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Differentiate between a track-chain real-time MIDI sequencer and an offline Live 12 MIDI Tool.","[ ] Configure <code>[live.step]</code> to output pitch, velocity, and duration tuples from a 16-step grid.","[ ] Demonstrate how a pseudo-random seed parameter guarantees repeatable algorithmic playback."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Max for Live Reference Manual: live.step object</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Max for Live Production Guidelines: Real-Time Sequencing</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: Deterministic Seeding</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"m4l-m-10-4-4","code":"10.4","title":"10.4","blurb":"10.4","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-10-4-1","title":"10.4.1: Advanced MIDI — MPE Processing and Passthrough","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 10.4.1","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 10 / Section 10.4","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 10.3.1 - Advanced MIDI — Algorithmic Step Sequencing.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual: <em>MPE (MIDI Polyphonic Expression)</em>.","Ableton Production Guidelines: <em>MPE Device Flags and Passthrough Requirements</em>.","Cycling '74: <em>Max 9 User Guide: Working with MPE Streams</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Explain the architectural structure of MIDI Polyphonic Expression (MPE) streams across per-note MIDI channels (2–16) and the Global Zone Manager (Channel 1).","Identify the three primary MPE per-note expression dimensions: <strong>Pitch Bend (Glide)</strong>, <strong>Pressure (Slide/Aftertouch)</strong>, and <strong>Timbre (CC 74 / Brightness)</strong>.","Configure the <code>is_mpe</code> patcher attribute in the Patcher Inspector to declare MPE support to Ableton Live.","Construct an MPE-safe MIDI effect that processes note pitch while preserving all concurrent per-note expression streams.","Prevent the accidental stripping of MPE multidimensional data when routing MIDI through high-level Max parsers."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 The MPE Channel Allocation Model","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Standard MIDI transmits pitch bend and CC globally across a single channel, affecting all held notes simultaneously. MPE (MIDI Polyphonic Expression) achieves polyphonic articulation by distributing individual notes across independent MIDI channels:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                            MPE CHANNEL TOPOLOGY                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  Master / Global Zone:  Channel 1  (Program changes, global sustain pedal)    |\n|                                                                               |\n|  Member Channels:       Channels 2 - 16                                       |\n|  - Channel 2: Note C3  + Per-Note Pitch Bend + Per-Note Pressure + CC 74      |\n|  - Channel 3: Note E3  + Per-Note Pitch Bend + Per-Note Pressure + CC 74      |\n|  - Channel 4: Note G3  + Per-Note Pitch Bend + Per-Note Pressure + CC 74      |\n|  - Channel 5: Note B3  + Per-Note Pitch Bend + Per-Note Pressure + CC 74      |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        MPE PER-NOTE EXPRESSION AXES                               |\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| Dimension         | MIDI Protocol Representation  | Typical Acoustic Target       |\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| **Glide (X-Axis)**| High-resolution Pitch Bend    | Per-note microtonal pitch     |\n|                   | on member channel (14-bit).   | sliding / vibrato.            |\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| **Press (Z-Axis)**| Channel Pressure / Aftertouch | Individual note dynamics, VCA,|\n|                   | on member channel (7-bit).    | distortion drive.             |\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| **Slide (Y-Axis)**| Continuous Controller 74      | Per-note filter brightness,   |\n|                   | (CC 74) on member channel.    | formant vowel, sample start.  |\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Declaring MPE Support: The is_mpe Attribute","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To ensure that Ableton Live routes multi-channel MPE streams into an <code>.amxd</code> device rather than collapsing them onto Channel 1:"},{"type":"list","items":["Open the root patcher in Max.","Open the <strong>Patcher Inspector</strong> (<code>Ctrl+Shift+I</code> / <code>Cmd+Shift+I</code>).","Locate the property <strong>MPE Supported</strong> / <code>is_mpe</code> and set it to <strong><code>1</code> (Enabled)</strong>.","When loaded in Live, the device title bar exposes MPE routing indicators, and Live passes MPE clip data into <code>[midiin]</code> with full channel fidelity."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|  Patcher Inspector: Root Patcher                                      |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|  MPE Supported (is_mpe):         [X] Enabled (1)                      |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. MPE Passthrough Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If a MIDI effect uses standard <code>[notein]</code> without accounting for MPE, it strips Pitch Bend and CC 74 from the stream. To build an MPE-compliant processor:"},{"type":"list","items":["Ingest raw serial bytes using <code>[midiin]</code>.","Use <code>[midiparse]</code> to extract note data on channels 2–16.","Apply transposition or scale algorithms to the pitch stream while preserving channel IDs.","Pass Pitch Bend, Pressure, and CC 74 directly through <code>[midiformat]</code> into <code>[midiout]</code>."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                    +---------------------------+\n                    |          midiin           |\n                    +-------------+-------------+\n                                  |\n                                  v\n                    +---------------------------+\n                    |         midiparse         |\n                    +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+\n                        |   |   |   |   |   |\n          (Note Pitch) -+   |   |   |   |   +-- (Pitch Bend Passthrough)\n          (Note Vel) -------+   |   |   +------ (Pressure Passthrough)\n          (Note Chan) ----------+   +---------- (CC 74 Passthrough)\n                |\n                v\n       [ Process Pitch ] (Preserve Channel ID!)\n                |\n                v\n    +-----------+-----------------------------------+\n    |                   midiformat                  |\n    +-----------------------+-----------------------+\n                            |\n                            v\n                    +---------------+\n                    |    midiout    |\n                    +---------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Construct an MPE-aware pitch transposer that shifts MIDI notes while preserving per-note pitch bends, pressure curves, and CC 74 slide trajectories."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, create a MIDI track, insert <strong>Drift</strong> (an MPE-capable instrument), and insert a <strong>Max MIDI Effect</strong> before it.","In the Max editor Patcher Inspector, enable <strong>MPE Supported</strong> (<code>is_mpe = 1</code>).","Create <code>[midiin]</code> connected to <code>[midiparse]</code>.","Create a <code>[live.dial]</code> named <code>MPE_Transpose</code> (Int, range: <code>-12 to +12</code>).","Process Notes:","Extract pitch, velocity, and channel from <code>[midiparse]</code> outlet 1.","Add <code>MPE_Transpose</code> to pitch using <code>[+ 0]</code>.","Re-route processed pitch, original velocity, and original channel to <code>[midiformat]</code> inlet 1.","Passthrough MPE Dimensions:","Connect outlet 2 of <code>[midiparse]</code> (CC data, including CC 74) → inlet 2 of <code>[midiformat]</code>.","Connect outlet 5 of <code>[midiparse]</code> (Channel Pressure) → inlet 5 of <code>[midiformat]</code>.","Connect outlet 6 of <code>[midiparse]</code> (Pitch Bend) → inlet 6 of <code>[midiformat]</code>.","Connect outlet 1 of <code>[midiformat]</code> to <code>[midiout]</code>.","Save device as <code>MPE_Safe_Transposer.amxd</code>.","In Live, draw an MPE clip containing per-note pitch glides and pressure slopes in Clip View.","Play back the clip: Verify that Drift receives transposed notes while executing full per-note polyphonic pitch sliding and pressure dynamics."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Forgetting <code>is_mpe = 1</code> in Inspector:</strong> Building complete MPE logic in the patcher but leaving the <code>is_mpe</code> attribute disabled in the Patcher Inspector. Live will automatically filter multi-channel MPE data to Channel 1 before sending it to the device.","<strong>Channel Flattening in <code>midiformat</code>:</strong> Leaving the channel inlet of <code>[midiformat]</code> unconnected, collapsing all outgoing notes onto Channel 1 and destroying per-note polyphonic articulation.","<strong>Filtering CC 74 Accidentally:</strong> Using standard CC filters that block controllers above CC 64, silently stripping MPE Slide information."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Self-Assessment & Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checkpoint Criteria","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To confirm mastery of this module, the student must successfully complete the following criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Diagram the 3-axis multidimensional control model used by MPE hardware controllers.","[ ] Locate and enable the <code>is_mpe</code> attribute in the Max Patcher Inspector.","[ ] Demonstrate complete polyphonic pitch-bend and pressure preservation through an <code>.amxd</code> MIDI effect."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"9. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton AG. (2024). <em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual: MIDI Polyphonic Expression (MPE)</em>. Berlin: Ableton AG.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Max for Live Production Guidelines: Final QA Checklist - MPE</em>, p. 68. Ableton Developer Documentation.","Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Max 9 User Guide: Multichannel and MPE Processing</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
